.Dd October 2, 2026
.Dt LIMITS 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm limits
.Nd process resource limits
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Each process has six resource limits.
Each has an enforced soft value and a hard value that bounds the soft value.
.Xr prlimit64 2
maintains them;
.Fn getrlimit ,
.Fn setrlimit
and, on i686, the kernel call
.Xr ugetrlimit 2
use the same table.
Fork inherits both values and exec preserves them, except that an exec
that runs a program with IDs its caller lacks, a set-user-ID or
set-group-ID program, lowers a soft
.Dv RLIMIT_STACK
above 8 MiB to 8 MiB, as Linux does.
.Bl -tag -width RLIMIT_NOFILE
.It Dv RLIMIT_NPROC
The number of threads with the same real user ID across the machine, each
thread of a process counting, as on Linux.
Fork and clone return
.Er EAGAIN
when the soft limit is reached.
Real user ID 0 is exempt.
A setuid operation that changes the real user ID moves the thread to the
new user's count.
.It Dv RLIMIT_NOFILE
One greater than the highest descriptor number the process may be given,
at most 1024, in a descriptor table it shares with another process as in
one of its own.
Open returns
.Er EMFILE
when no descriptor below the soft limit is free.
Dup2 returns
.Er EBADF
for a destination at or above the soft limit.
The descriptor table starts at sixteen entries and grows to 1024.
.It Dv RLIMIT_AS
The bytes covered by the process's virtual memory regions.
Growth past the soft limit makes mmap return
.Er ENOMEM
and leaves the break unchanged when brk fails.
.It Dv RLIMIT_STACK
The bytes of the stack an exec gives a program, 8 MiB soft by default.
.It Dv RLIMIT_CORE
The bytes of a core file, 0 soft by default, which leaves none, as
.Xr core 5
describes.
.It Dv RLIMIT_FSIZE
The bytes a file the process writes may reach, no limit by default.
A write that would pass the soft limit writes the bytes below it, and one
that starts at the limit fails.
That write, a truncation past the limit and an
.Xr fallocate 2
that would grow the file past it fail with
.Er EFBIG
and send
.Dv SIGXFSZ
to the calling thread.
.El
.Ss Initial limits
Init reads
.Pa /etc/rach2/limits
at boot and sets its own limits before starting children.
Every session inherits these limits.
A missing file leaves the kernel defaults.
.Pp
Each line has a name,
.Ql nproc ,
.Ql nofile
or
.Ql as ,
followed by the soft and hard values.
Values are decimal numbers or
.Ql unlimited .
Address-space values are bytes.
The os-real image supplies:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nproc 256 512
nofile 1024 1024
as unlimited unlimited
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Ic ulimit
built-in in
.Xr sh 1
reads and lowers limits.
.Ss Other limits
Process records are allocated from the kernel heap.
If the kernel cannot allocate a process record, fork returns
.Er ENOMEM .
Sockets and TCP control blocks also come from the heap, and so do a
descriptor table's growth and each open-file description: a call that
makes a descriptor returns
.Er ENOMEM
when the heap has no room for them, and
.Er EMFILE
only at the descriptor limit.
These allocations are outside the resource-limit table.
Other fixed capacities are the loopback ring, 64 frames;
the USB device table, sixteen records; and the disk registry, eight records.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/rach2/limits
.It Pa /etc/rach2/limits
Initial soft and hard limits read by init.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Dl cat /etc/rach2/limits
.Pp
The default test suite's
.Nm limitprobe
checks the refusals of the process, descriptor and address-space limits
by name.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sh 1 ,
.Xr prlimit64 2 ,
.Xr core 5 ,
.Xr memory 7
